      "dom_id": "question-140145",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 2-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )+15) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+11) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}+11=0,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Incorrect",
